 "batch_notes": "rust/jj has two distinct properties sharing the id rust/jj::test_parse_program_never_panics (fileset_parser and revset variants); both are listed, so this batch has 122 entries but 121 distinct ids. Recurring shape the rubric handles only implicitly: a general property that gates around a pinned bug (tc.assume / early return / generator narrowed) with a comment naming the pin - present in jj, json5, jsonc-parser, ketos, kiddo, koto, kurbo, lenient_semver and lintian-overrides; I used those citations as basis=explicit per rule 2, and the lintian-overrides README's list of narrowed generators as basis=inferred. Resource-limit properties (ketos restrict_call_stack_bounds_recursion, deep_nesting_with_restrict_is_rejected, jsonc-parser pbt_deep_nesting_rejected_not_crashed) assert 'a configured limit yields an error and no native overflow' - I filed them under rejection but they sit between rejection and robustness. Host-language-as-oracle for embedded languages (koto arithmetic/comparison/concatenation vs Rust) is ambiguous between model-oracle (the rubric's 'exact arithmetic' clause) and external-oracle (stdlib); I chose model-oracle because the reference is a test-written evaluator/rule. kurbo pathseg_eval_at_endpoints_matches_start_and_end is documented KNOWN FAILURE with a boosted extreme-magnitude branch but remains the general full-range property, so it is not tagged pin; kiddo's two *_known_bug tests are narrowed and are. Quality: jotdown, json5 (README), jsonc-parser, ketos, kiddo, koto have no README doc lines ('(no doc comment)'), so classification relied on bodies; json5 and kiddo have rich in-source comments the README does not carry. lintian-overrides README head is truncated at 2500 chars in the batch, hiding its Bugs/Notes sections that name the narrowed generators; I read the target README to attribute."
